   "note": "The unit converter (reference wing, sibling to moji + uni). Thin assets Worker (worker unit, custom_domain unit.mino.mobi) - no build/D1/AI/secrets… Facts Machine-readable entry: deploy-registry.json → surfaces[] where surface == \"unit\". How it works The unit converter (reference wing, sibling to moji + uni). Thin assets Worker (worker unit, custom_domain unit.mino.mobi) - no build/D1/AI/secrets. Home: category dropdown then two iOS-style CYLINDER unit pickers (unit/lib/cylinder.js, scroll-snap drum) flanking a value input + swap, with a live FULL-SPECTRUM table converting the value into every unit at once (click a row to set it as the target). Deep-linkable at /<category>/<from>/<to>?v= (e.g. /length/meter/foot?v=1). /reference is the longform table page: every unit's exact factor to/from its base both ways, affine temperature formulas, non-linear fuel economy, and SI + IEC binary prefixes. ~15 categories; all conversions in the pure engine unit/lib/units.js (factor/offset, or toBase/fromBase fns for non-affine), node-tested by unit/lib/units.selftest.mjs. Currency deliberately excluded (needs live rates). /color (aliases /colour, /light, /wavelength; deep-linkable at /color/589.3 or\n/color?hex=ff00ff) is the wavelength ↔ color page: a splash, a spectrum strip\npainted from the engine, linked nm/THz/eV fields, a native color picker with\nthe EyeDropper API where it exists, a canvas CIE 1931 chromaticity diagram, and\nthe landmark spectral lines. Its engine is unit/lib/spectrum.js — separate from\nunits.js on purpose, because this is not a unit conversion (see below) —\nnode-tested by unit/lib/spectrum.selftest.mjs. The color page's two asymmetries Both of these look like bugs and are not. Read this before \"fixing\" either. 1. **A color has no wavelength.** Only the rim of the CIE horseshoe is pure light. Everything inside is a mixture, so color → nm returns a *dominant* wavelength: cast a ray from D65 white through the color's chromaticity and see where it leaves the locus. Greys have no direction at all (achromatic), and magentas leave through the line of purples, which no wavelength touches (purple) — those get a complementary wavelength instead. That is why the page is two cards, not a swap button. 2. **No screen can show a spectral color.** Every point on the locus is outside the sRGB gamut, so S.rgb(nm) has to bring it in, and purity reports how far short it fell (cyan is worst, ~21%). Two mappings: mode: 'vivid' (default) clips the negative primaries — the familiar poster spectrum, but past sRGB's red primary at 611 nm every wavelength renders identically; mode: 'true' adds white instead, which keeps the dominant wavelength exact so nm → color → nm round-trips, at the cost of visibly pink deep reds. The page shows vivid and quotes the true chip beside it. Two more places the physics bites, both handled in spectrum.js: past 700 nm the\nstandard observer's x̄/ȳ ratio is constant, so the locus stops moving and hue\nsaturates (the ray-cast locus therefore ends at 700, S.LOCUS_MAX); and 8-bit\nrounding is coarser than the purple wedge is thin at the red tip, so a hit\nwithin PURPLE_SNAP of a terminus is snapped back onto the locus. Color-matching data is the CIE 1931 2° standard observer at 5 nm, 380–780,\nlinearly interpolated — 81 rows inlined in spectrum.js, from CVRL's\nciexyz31.csv. The selftest pins it against published chromaticities (700 nm →\n0.73469, 0.26531) and against the published dominant wavelengths of the sRGB\nprimaries (611.4 / 549.1 / 464.2 nm), so a transposed matrix or a mangled table\nfails loudly. /chroma — color as sound /chroma (aliases /sound, /hear, /synesthesia, /synaesthesia; deep links\n/chroma/532, /chroma?hex=ff00ff) sonifies a spectrum. Engine\nunit/lib/chroma.js, which imports spectrum.js for the CIE observer rather\nthan keeping a second copy of it; node-tested by chroma.selftest.mjs. Web\nAudio lives only in the page — the engine is pure, so the physics is testable. **The fact the page is built on.** Visible light spans 384–789 THz, a ratio of\n2.05: the entire rainbow is **one octave**. Divide by 2^40 and it lands on\nF4 → F5. That is not a tuning choice, it is the whole subject. **Three things that look like bugs and are not:** 1. **A broad spectrum is a cluster, not a chord.** One octave is 1200 cents and the band is 1245. Chop it into 48 and partials sit 26 cents apart — a quarter of a semitone, inside the range where the ear hears beating instead of pitch. No mapping avoids this. The stretch control buys resolution by lying about the frequency and is labelled as such.\n2. **Roughness is measured over a FIXED bank.** Every source is played through the same log-spaced frequencies; a color changes only their gains. Sample a spectrum more finely and a naive partial list gets mechanically rougher, so the score would measure the setting instead of the color. It is normalised by total amplitude *squared* — pairs grow as N², so anything linear in N leaves N in the answer. bank() also means the page never rebuilds its oscillators mid-drag.\n3. **Screen white is not a clean triad.** Real emitters are ~25 nm wide, which is ±160 cents, so each is already a cluster. What you hear is three *lumps* in a rough band. lobes() names the humps; \"humps only\" plays the tidy idealisation instead, for contrast. **The emitter model.** PRIMARIES are the narrowest single bumps whose\nluminance-weighted mix reproduces D65 within 0.005 in xy — the selftest checks\nthat by integrating them back through spectrum.js's observer. Note that no\nsingle emitter *can* be an sRGB primary: (0.64, 0.33) sits inside the spectral\nlocus, so a red bump overshoots to about (0.655, 0.345). Real emitters are more\nsaturated than sRGB asks for, which is why wide-gamut displays exist. **The metamer pair is solved, not approximated.** A blackbody cannot match\nscreen white — the Planckian locus runs below the daylight locus, leaving 0.007\nof residual tint, which is visible side by side. So metamerOf() takes three\nbroad Gaussians and solves the 3×3 system for the weights reproducing the\ntarget's XYZ *exactly* (agreement to ~1e-16), rejecting any solution that would\nneed negative light. Three spikes and one smooth hill, same color to the last\ndecimal, completely different sound. Do not \"simplify\" this back to a\ntemperature; that was tried and it is wrong.",
