   "desc": "An agent that weaves orb webs with no plan and no map — it holds eight numbers about its own body and reads everything else off the silk it has already hung, which makes the half-built web its scaffolding, its ruler and its memory at once. That is Physarum’s trick with a different medium, and the difference is the whole point: a chemical trail evaporates, so a slime mould can forget a bad early commitment; silk cannot be un-laid. So one set of boundary conditions admits not a web but a family — sixteen seeds agree on capture area and spiral turns to within two percent while sharing no thread position at all — and nudging one decision does damage in proportion to how early it was, two orders of magnitude from the bridge line down to the four-hundredth spiral attachment. Turn gravity off and the hub centres, which is the control that makes the rest a result. word is the same shape asked a different question: all 39,554 word types from a 50,000-post Bluesky archive, placed by topic, rank and date — and honest about being a bad chart, with the Zipf and Heaps plots that actually work sitting underneath it.",
